// ENG4U 19 The Sustained Literary-Analysis Essay. Source: /Users/ali/Desktop/MV/Modules/eng4u/plan/modules/19-sustained-essay.md
// Texts: the student's own ISU novel (typed by the student; never reproduced); an original model essay written for the course
// ("Made, Not Born"); short public-domain quotations from Frankenstein (1831 text), Jane Eyre (1847), The Great Gatsby (1925),
// Pride and Prejudice (1813), Dracula (1897), Heart of Darkness (1899) and Bacon's "Of Studies" (1625). Practice theses, outlines,
// paragraphs and the task models are original to the course.
// Quotations checked 2026-11-26 against Project Gutenberg #42324 (Frankenstein, 1831 text; "Every where" is the 1831 spelling),
// #1260 (Jane Eyre; ch. 27 "I care for myself" has "I" in italics in the original) and Wikisource "The Great Gatsby (1925)";
// wording, punctuation and chapter numbers match.

const T_FR = "<em>Frankenstein</em>", T_JE = "<em>Jane Eyre</em>", T_GG = "<em>The Great Gatsby</em>";
const RUB = "<details class='alg se-rubric'><summary>How your teacher reads the writing (levels 1–4)</summary><div class='tbl-wrap'><table class='topic-tbl'><thead><tr><th scope='col'>Category</th><th scope='col'>Level 1</th><th scope='col'>Level 2</th><th scope='col'>Level 3</th><th scope='col'>Level 4</th></tr></thead><tbody>" +
  "<tr><td>Knowledge and Understanding</td><td>Retells the novel; terms misused</td><td>Some understanding; analysis is thin</td><td>Clear understanding of the novel and of essay form</td><td>Thorough, nuanced understanding of the novel and of form</td></tr>" +
  "<tr><td>Thinking</td><td>Claim absent or factual; evidence random</td><td>Claim present but loosely supported</td><td>Arguable claim; relevant evidence, analysed</td><td>Insightful claim; evidence analysed closely, with counterargument handled</td></tr>" +
  "<tr><td>Communication</td><td>Unclear organization; quotations dropped; citations missing</td><td>Some organization; frequent integration or citation errors</td><td>Logical organization; integrated quotations; mostly correct MLA</td><td>Coherent, purposeful line; elegant integration; precise MLA and style</td></tr>" +
  "<tr><td>Application</td><td>No connection beyond the passage</td><td>A simple connection</td><td>Connects the evidence to the thesis and the novel’s larger concerns</td><td>Extends the argument to wider significance with originality</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details>";

// The model essay (original). Paragraph 3's topic sentence opens with "Yet" so the essay's transitions can be counted (see deviations).
const ESSAY = [
  { id:"p1", label:"Introduction", ts:"",
    t:"Early in his own story, the Creature sees his face in a pool for the first time and recoils. He has done nothing yet; he has only looked. He has learned what beauty is from the family he watches through a chink in the wall, and he has learned it well enough to condemn himself: in that instant he becomes, in his own words, “the monster that I am” (Shelley, ch. 12). The reflection is a small model of the whole novel, because the judgment arrives before any deed. In <em>Frankenstein</em>, Mary Shelley shows that monstrousness is not a fact of the Creature’s body but a verdict passed on him by others, and by tracing how rejection turns his eloquence into rage, the novel asks whether Victor’s true failure was his science or his refusal to care." },
  { id:"p2", label:"Paragraph 2",
    ts:"The Creature first speaks not as a fiend but as a reader, and Shelley makes his eloquence the clearest evidence of his humanity.",
    t:"The Creature first speaks not as a fiend but as a reader, and Shelley makes his eloquence the clearest evidence of his humanity. Hidden beside the cottage, he learns language by listening, and then he learns the world through books: <em>Paradise Lost</em>, Plutarch’s <em>Lives</em> and <em>The Sorrows of Werter</em>. When he finally tells his story to Victor, he speaks in long, balanced sentences that rival his maker’s own, and he even reaches for Milton to describe himself, insisting that he ought to be Victor’s Adam. Shelley gives the supposed monster the novel’s most moving voice, so the reader hears a mind long before anyone describes a face. That order matters. A reader who has spent chapters inside the Creature’s reasoning cannot easily call him a beast, and the novel quietly makes us into the patient listener that no character in the story will agree to be." },
  { id:"p3", label:"Paragraph 3",
    ts:"Yet each rejection the Creature suffers is staged as a judgment of his appearance, never of his actions.",
    t:"Yet each rejection the Creature suffers is staged as a judgment of his appearance, never of his actions. Victor flees from him in the first hour of his life, before the Creature has done anything at all. Villagers drive him off with stones for stepping into a hut. The cottagers he has secretly helped for months, gathering their firewood at night, scatter in terror the moment they see him, and Felix strikes him before he can finish his plea. When he saves a girl from drowning, the man with her answers with a gunshot. In every scene the sequence is the same: the Creature acts kindly or speaks well, and the onlookers respond only to his face. The verdict comes before the evidence, which is exactly what makes it a verdict and not a discovery." },
  { id:"p4", label:"Paragraph 4",
    ts:"When the Creature declares, “I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend” (Shelley, ch. 10), he names the cause-and-effect the novel has been building.",
    t:"When the Creature declares, “I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend” (Shelley, ch. 10), he names the cause-and-effect the novel has been building. The sentence turns on its semicolon: before it, a past of goodness; after it, a present made by suffering. The verb <em>made</em> is the key. It echoes Victor’s own act of making, as if misery has finished the work that Victor abandoned. The Creature does not deny that he has become a fiend; he denies that he was born one. Shelley lets him argue his own case, and the argument persuades precisely because it is so orderly: a premise, a turning point and a conclusion, the shape of a defence in a courtroom." },
  { id:"p5", label:"Paragraph 5 (counterargument)",
    ts:"Some readers argue that the Creature’s murders cancel his claim to sympathy, and his violence is real; yet Shelley places each crime after an act of abandonment, so the reader must weigh the deeds against their causes.",
    t:"Some readers argue that the Creature’s murders cancel his claim to sympathy, and his violence is real; yet Shelley places each crime after an act of abandonment, so the reader must weigh the deeds against their causes. William dies soon after the Creature has been shot for saving a life. Clerval and Elizabeth die after Victor tears apart the half-made companion the Creature was promised. None of this excuses the killings, and Shelley never asks us to forgive them. She asks for something harder: to see that every blow is struck by a being who has been refused, at each step, the one thing he asked for. The murders do not disprove the thesis; they are its darkest evidence." },
  { id:"p6", label:"Conclusion", ts:"",
    t:"<em>Frankenstein</em> is often read as a warning about forbidden knowledge, but Shelley’s warning reaches further. Victor’s science made a body; his refusal to care made a monster. By the end, maker and made have become hunter and hunted, each pursuing the other across the ice, and the duty Victor refused at the beginning is the only thing that could have stopped the chase. The novel asks what we owe to whatever we make: a child, an invention, a stranger at the door. It closes as the Creature leaves Walton’s ship, carried off by the waves and “lost in darkness and distance” (Shelley, ch. 24), a verdict no one stayed to revise." }
];
const DRIFT = "The Alps scenes show Shelley’s love of the sublime. Mont Blanc’s glaciers are described in vivid detail, and Victor feels calm among the mountains. Romantic writers often found peace in nature.";

